/-!
# The derived category of adequate modules does not depend on the topology

Let `S` be a scheme and let `τ` and `τ'` be two of the five topologies `Zar`, `étale`, `smooth`,
`syntomic`, `fppf`, with `τ` stronger than `τ'`. The structure sheaf `𝒪` of the big `τ`-site
over `S` is also the structure sheaf of the big `τ'`-site on the same category of schemes over
`S`, and the derived categories `D_Adeq(𝒪_τ)` and `D_Adeq(𝒪_τ')` of complexes of `𝒪`-modules
with adequate cohomology are canonically equivalent.

This is [Stacks, Tag 07A8] for the category `𝒞` of the big site, the class `𝒜` of adequate
modules for `τ` and the collection `ℬ` of affine objects. Its four assumptions hold:

1. an adequate module for `τ'` is a sheaf for `τ`, indeed for the fpqc topology
   ([Stacks, Tag 06VL]), and every covering family of `τ` is an fpqc covering family;
2. the adequate modules form a weak Serre subcategory ([Stacks, Tag 06VQ]);
3. every object has a `τ'`-covering by affine objects, by an affine open covering of its scheme;
4. the higher cohomology of an adequate module on an affine object vanishes
   ([Stacks, Tag 06VM]).

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_ofArrows_le_of_mem_overTopology` and
  `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.exists_ofArrows_le_of_mem_topology`: a covering sieve of a big
  `τ`-site contains a family of morphisms of the site which is a `τ`-covering of schemes.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.le_topology_of_isFiner`: along the chain
  `Zar ≺ étale ≺ smooth ≺ syntomic ≺ fppf`, the topology of the coarser of two big sites on the
  same category is contained in that of the finer one.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.overTopology_le_fpqc`: the topology of a localized big site is coarser than
  the topology its fpqc covering families generate.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.topology_le_fpqcPrecoverageOn`: the topology of a chosen big site is
  coarser than the topology generated by its fpqc covering families.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.Coarsening.adequateIsFineSheaf`: assumption (1) of [Stacks, Tag 07A8]
  for the adequate modules.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.Coarsening.affineCoarseCovering`: assumption (3) of
  [Stacks, Tag 07A8] for the affine objects.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.Coarsening.derivedAdequateCoarseningEquivalence`:
  `D_Adeq(𝒪_τ) ≌ D_Adeq(𝒪_τ')` for a coarsening of a chosen big `τ`-site.
* `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.derivedAdequateTopologyEquivalence`: **[Stacks, Tag 070V]**,
  `D_Adeq(𝒪_τ) ≌ D_Adeq(𝒪_τ')` for any two of the five topologies.

## Implementation notes

*The two topologies on one category.* As in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Adequate/AdequateModules/TopologyIndependence.lean`, "`τ` is
stronger than `τ'`" is a `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite.Coarsening` of the chosen big `τ`-site: a
second topology, contained in the first, on the same category, which is the topology of a chosen
big `τ'`-site with the same cutoff. The source takes `𝒞 = (Sch/S)_fppf` and puts both topologies
on it; the category of a chosen big site plays that role here.

*Any two topologies.* The big sites `(Sch/S)_τ` of the source are all built on one category of
schemes over `S`. A chosen big site `Stacks.Adequate.BigSite τ S` fixes its category by its
cutoff, so two chosen big sites with equal cutoffs have the same category, and the registered
statement asks for that equality. The five topologies are linearly ordered, and for `τ` finer
than `τ'` the `τ'`-site is a coarsening of the `τ`-site: a covering sieve of the `τ'`-site
contains a `τ'`-covering family, which is a `τ`-covering family ([Stacks, Tag 021N]). The other
case is the same equivalence, inverted.

*Sizes.* The objects of a chosen big site lie one universe above its morphisms, and the site is
essentially small, so [Stacks, Tag 07A8] is applied in the form
`Stacks.Cohomology.CompareTopologies.derivedEquivalenceImageOfLEOfEssentiallySmall`. The modules
have values in the universe of the morphisms of the site, as in [Stacks, Tag 06VQ].

*The two derived categories.* They are taken at arbitrary models `HasDerivedCategory`, one for
each topology. The equivalence is one of categories, which is the assertion of the remark.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 070V](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/070V)
